{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788353020,"nonce":"XxdjHMAT5+5aIG4FAC5zNq5t49g=","message":"Established from the run's OWN saves in `RUNS/m1-20260901/saves/` and from the\ndecompiled 1.6 source. Every number below is re-derived offline by\n`accept/eef837a-bill-filter.py --selftest`, phase 9, so none of it has to be\ntaken on my word.\n\n\n`RimWorld/BillUtility.cs MakeNewBill` ends, in every branch, in the\n`Bill(RecipeDef, Precept_ThingStyle)` ctor:\n\n```csharp\ningredientFilter = new ThingFilter();\ningredientFilter.CopyAllowancesFrom(recipe.defaultIngredientFilter);\n```\n\nand `Verse/RecipeDef.cs ResolveReferences` guarantees `defaultIngredientFilter`\nis non-null (where the XML omits it, the game builds one and copies\n`fixedIngredientFilter` into it). `bill-add` already called `MakeNewBill`, so it\nalready produced exactly what the Add Bill button produces.\n\n**The artifact agrees.** `day-46.rws` — the save from the day the run created\nthat bill (`loadID 6`) — has its `\u003callowedDefs\u003e` at **115 entries, every one an\nanimal corpse**: `Corpse_WildBoar` present, `Corpse_Human` absent, no mechanoid\ncorpse. That is `ButcherCorpseFlesh.defaultIngredientFilter` (`CorpsesAnimal`)\nexactly. The filter was correct for the entire run.\n\nWhat was missing was any way to SEE that. `bill-add` now returns a `diagnosis`\nblock carrying `filter_defs` and `recipe_default_defs` side by side, so the\nequality is a published assertion rather than an inference — and a Harmony patch\nor a modded `MakeNewBill` override that ever broke it shows up in the verb's own\nresult.\n\n\n`bills` publishes `ingredient_filter`, never `filter`. It was set inside a bare\n`try/catch` as the LAST statement of `ColonyVerbs.BillLine`, so a throw out of\n`FilterSummary` left the key **absent**, and `FilterSummary.Build` returns\n**null** for a null `bill.ingredientFilter`. Absent and null are the same thing\nto every consumer. Three different facts — \"this bill has no ingredientFilter\",\n\"the summary failed\", \"the filter is empty\" — arrived as one indistinguishable\n`null`, and the run drew the wrong conclusion from it and spent its last days\nchasing a filter that was fine.\n\n`filter_state` is now `published | empty | absent | unavailable`, unconditional,\nbeside an `ingredient_filter` that is a dict whenever it is published.\n\n\n`ButcherCorpseFlesh.fixedIngredientFilter` carries\n\n```xml\n\u003cspecialFiltersToDisallow\u003e\u003cli\u003eAllowRotten\u003c/li\u003e\u003c/specialFiltersToDisallow\u003e\n```\n\n`SpecialThingFilterWorker_Rotten.Matches` is `CompRottable.Stage != Fresh`, it\nis evaluated **per THING** by `Verse/ThingFilter.cs Allows(Thing)`'s last\nclause, and `RimWorld/Bill.cs IsFixedOrAllowedIngredient` consults\n`recipe.fixedIngredientFilter` **before** the bill's own filter. So a rotting\ncorpse is rejected at the RECIPE, and no `bill-set` lever can widen past it at\nany price.\n\n`day-62.rws` has `Corpse_WildBoar` at `(114, 0, 138)` — the butcher spot's own\ncell — `forbidden` absent, `rotProg 183767`. `CompRottable`'s rot start for a\nflesh corpse is 2.5 days = 150,000 ticks (`ThingDefGenerator_Corpses`). **The\ncorpse this issue calls \"fresh (hp 95%)\" was already rotting.** Hit points are\nnot rot, and nothing published said so.\n\nDay-by-day from the saves, the whole failure is legible: on day 60 there were\nthree FRESH unforbidden corpses (`Corpse_WildBoar (97,81)`, `Corpse_Deer\n(232,18)`, `Corpse_Donkey (109,181)`); by day 62 all three were past rot start;\nfrom day 62 to the wipe every unforbidden corpse on the map was Rotting or\nDessicated and the one Fresh one (`Corpse_Hare`, day 66) was forbidden. The bill\nwas right the whole time. The colony had a ~48-hour window on day 60–61 and\nnothing told it so.\n\n**So the fix is not to the write path. It is that a def-level filter summary is\nnot an answer about a thing.** Every bill line now carries `ingredient_match`,\nwhich runs `WorkGiver_DoBill`'s own predicate — `TryFindBestIngredientsHelper`'s\n`baseValidator` over `IsUsableIngredient` — clause by clause over the things\nactually on the map and NAMES the clause that rejected each:\n`recipe-fixed:special:AllowRotten`, `bill-filter:def-not-allowed`, `forbidden`,\n`out-of-search-radius`, `hit-points-out-of-range`, and so on. `rejected_sample`\nrows carry the thing id, the cell, `forbidden`, and `rot_stage`. `health` folds\nit into one word and `remedy` names the verb — including the case where the\nhonest answer is **\"NO BILL LEVER FIXES THIS\"**, which is the sentence that\nwould have changed this run.\n\nReachability and reservation are pawn-scoped and are NOT answered; the block\nsays so in `clauses_not_checked` rather than implying a pass. Forbidding is\nanswered at the FACTION level and `forbidden_scope` says that too.\n\n\n`bill-set {allow:[\"Corpses\"]}` DID persist: `day-66.rws` has the bill at 127\ndefs, up from 115. What did not persist is the NUMBER the verb reported.\n115 + 39 = 154, which is exactly the `allowed_defs: 154` in this issue — so the\nreport was internally consistent and wrong. The save holds 127: **27 of the 39\nevaporated**, and what evaporated is exactly the mechanoid and drone corpse defs\nthat `fixedIngredientFilter.disallowedCategories` names. `Corpse_Human` and\n`UnnaturalCorpse_Human` survived; no `*Mech*` corpse did.\n\nCause: an asymmetry inside `StorageFilterOps.Toggle`. Its per-DEF path already\nrefused a def outside the parent filter, citing\n`Listing_TreeThingFilter.Visible` — the widget draws no row, so the player has\nno such checkbox. Its per-CATEGORY path did not, on the recorded argument that\n\"the allowance is dead for anything the parent rejects, so this matches vanilla\nexactly\". For a storage filter that is true. For a bill it is false: the\nallowance is **deleted** by `Bill.ExposeData`'s saving pass.\n\nFixed with the game's own parameter rather than an invented one —\n`ThingFilter.SetAllow(ThingCategoryDef, bool, exceptedDefs, exceptedFilters)`\ntakes the excluded set as its third argument, and `Listing_TreeThingFilter`\npasses `forceHiddenDefs` there for the same reason. `clampCategories` is ON for\nbills, OFF for storage (no ExposeData narrowing applies there and its behaviour\nmust not move). Withheld defs become `refused` lines naming the mechanism;\n`defs_delta` is now the delta that survives a save; `defs_withheld` says what\ndid not; and `will_not_persist` re-runs ExposeData's predicate as a QUESTION\nafter every write, so the claim is asserted per call rather than trusted.\n\nDeliberately NOT clamped on `Visible`'s other two clauses (`!PlayerAcquirable`,\n`virtualDefParent != null`): those hide a ROW but do not decide what survives a\nsave, and this code path writes a live filter.\n\n\nSee the comment on `d9d6c12`. Short version: the back-off is 500–600 TICKS and\nrearms on every failure, so it cannot \"sit in the future for days\"; the fix is a\nnamed state plus a sampled consecutive-failure count, and the two asleep\nverdicts (`asleep-will-retry` vs `asleep-no-matching-ingredient`) are different\nwords.\n\n\n`accept/eef837a-bill-filter.py`, nine phases. `--selftest` runs phase 9 offline\nand re-derives every constant out of the shipped source, the decompiled source\nand `Core/Defs/RecipeDefs/Recipes_Butchery.xml`, then reads the run's own saves\nfor the three numbers above. **28/28 offline.** Phases 1–6 and 8 need a bench and\nhave NOT been run — no worker launches RimWorld.\n\n\n`prioritize` still takes a `WorkGiverDef` (not a work type) and still requires\n`thing` or `cell`; phase 4 asserts both refusals and that the third form carries\nits `rejected` diagnostic. A follow-up is filed for the one gap that remains\nthere: its `blocked:` string is the game's own `MissingMaterials` translation\n(\"Missing 1x corpses\") and still does not say WHY.","files":null}]}